d}\n$$\n\nSince ${86.19}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={86.19}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{86.19}^{∘}={266.19}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx={86}^{∘},{266}^{∘}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533917 true] [17592186265691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265692 13194139533917 true] [17592186265691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265693 13194139533917 true] [17592186265691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265694 13194139533917 true] [17592186265691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265695 13194139533917 true] [17592186265691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265696 13194139533917 true] [17592186265691 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533917 true] [17592186265691 1041 "selection" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265691 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265695 13194139533917 true] [17592186265691 1110 #uuid "0d0979c4-a6bf-5ef0-9b2b-caa1f7fff0d4" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265692 1034 "x={93}^{∘},{273}^{∘}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265692 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265692 1111 #uuid "cf29bd25-be25-5596-a87c-7bca5ce573fd" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265693 1034 "x={80}^{∘},{260}^{∘}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265693 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265693 1111 #uuid "85557900-af23-574e-85ae-7ab0032c6bcd" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265694 1034 "x={71}^{∘},{251}^{∘}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265694 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265694 1111 #uuid "9144e89e-5100-5e9d-8661-2edd52be6f10" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265695 1034 "x={86}^{∘},{266}^{∘}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265695 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265695 1111 #uuid "973efc20-dfb3-520d-9663-e910b0c8d731" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265696 1034 "x={74}^{∘},{254}^{∘}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265696 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265696 1111 #uuid "9f93e05f-9fac-5ad6-ad61-3991aeed37b2" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265697 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265698 13194139533917 true] [17592186265697 1089 "q-121332" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265697 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533917 true] [17592186265697 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1=4$  for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265697 1113 #uuid "09744146-6a55-5a6c-9779-7507baea6402" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265697 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1 & =4 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={30}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=-{180}^{∘}+{x}_{1}=-{180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}=-{150}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-{150}^{∘},{30}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-150\\cdot 30=-4\\,500.\n$$" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265699 13194139533917 true] [17592186265698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265700 13194139533917 true] [17592186265698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265701 13194139533917 true] [17592186265698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265702 13194139533917 true] [17592186265698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265703 13194139533917 true] [17592186265698 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533917 true] [17592186265698 1041 "selection" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265698 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265703 13194139533917 true] [17592186265698 1110 #uuid "39335906-7965-5b7b-b39c-0f411fbdcdcb" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265699 1034 "-1\\,600" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265699 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265699 1111 #uuid "a94023f5-c2de-53f3-99d1-0122ee6a1352" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265700 1034 "-5\\,300" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265700 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265700 1111 #uuid "c7f414ec-c23e-5c94-a1ef-cc5ac0d12b70" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265701 1034 "-8\\,300" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265701 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265701 1111 #uuid "bef1c2d1-4e66-59d1-bdaf-9f5f726d2934" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265702 1034 "-3\\,600" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265702 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265702 1111 #uuid "81efa90b-43dc-5032-80c8-9e3077034f82" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265703 1034 "-4\\,500" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265703 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265703 1111 #uuid "994c292f-1380-58e3-bc58-04f84532ce4e" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265704 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265705 13194139533917 true] [17592186265704 1089 "q-121545" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265704 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533917 true] [17592186265704 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952589/assets/0d956ac6b75c38bfbc5650966880a4eedaf69a0659407fb137d88d30fea08ab7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\sqrt{3}.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the point $A.$\n\n$$\n\n$$" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265704 1113 #uuid "57cf5fd6-82f4-5843-a107-b49508195a40" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265704 1145 "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside $-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<0.$ Hence, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ This is the $x$ coordinate of $B.$\n\nTo get another solution, we add $-\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\pi +x=-\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265706 13194139533917 true] [17592186265705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265707 13194139533917 true] [17592186265705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265708 13194139533917 true] [17592186265705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265709 13194139533917 true] [17592186265705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265710 13194139533917 true] [17592186265705 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533917 true] [17592186265705 1041 "selection" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265705 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265708 13194139533917 true] [17592186265705 1110 #uuid "843d392a-3e80-5b0d-b9e3-6e9c678a8807" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265706 1034 "-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265706 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265706 1111 #uuid "ce68ef0f-3cb2-5124-85b3-d207736d1ca3" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265707 1034 "-\\frac{6\\pi }{5}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265707 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265707 1111 #uuid "f2ab487f-304a-5b85-8e84-4a65202a28aa" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265708 1034 "-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265708 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265708 1111 #uuid "f5fa732c-faac-5309-917d-db998fef904d" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265709 1034 "-\\pi " 13194139533917 true] [17592186265709 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265709 1111 #uuid "ae4b1db6-69f0-5280-b7f3-38aa8d055c17" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265710 1034 "-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265710 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265710 1111 #uuid "d369c9f5-9662-5cd1-868d-408562a68b4e" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265711 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265712 13194139533917 true] [17592186265711 1089 "q-20482" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265711 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533917 true] [17592186265711 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952589/assets/95be2e7bf1dc425e0f0dd9ba818a6a06d0c9afc7874213194f68b1dd426cb110.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=1.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the point $B.$\n\n$$\n\n$$" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265711 1113 #uuid "7f77a0be-54c9-59a4-987f-02f55232c640" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265711 1145 "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside $0\\le x\\le 2\\pi $ hence, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ This is the $x$ coordinate of $A.$\n\nTo get another solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $B$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265713 13194139533917 true] [17592186265712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265714 13194139533917 true] [17592186265712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265715 13194139533917 true] [17592186265712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265716 13194139533917 true] [17592186265712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265717 13194139533917 true] [17592186265712 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533917 true] [17592186265712 1041 "selection" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265712 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265713 13194139533917 true] [17592186265712 1110 #uuid "5366fc1f-4ae5-5a1a-bf03-f118b1c17f0b" 13