2c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$\n\nThe **exact** value of the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ is \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533917 true] [17592186265777 1113 #uuid "f3caba22-9915-504e-bd4c-02a454ebfe13" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265777 1145 "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $-\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =x+(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265779 13194139533917 true] [17592186265778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265780 13194139533917 true] [17592186265778 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533917 true] [17592186265778 1041 "field-1" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265778 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265780 13194139533917 true] [17592186265778 1110 #uuid "355a0cc2-1961-59cb-a988-f660dbf176cc" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265779 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265779 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265779 1111 #uuid "8514006d-99ea-5161-9f37-eb9f13dbd801" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265780 1034 "-\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265780 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265780 1111 #uuid "82c42992-a302-59ce-8ec3-ea9527c718f7" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265781 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265782 13194139533917 true] [17592186265781 1089 "q-322701" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265781 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533917 true] [17592186265781 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952589/assets/e03611d72e1c88d1e49806ede117b6808186de27346e426104dca69391f29bbf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$\n\nThe **exact** value of the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ is \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533917 true] [17592186265781 1113 #uuid "fa7635fe-efad-5976-a9f0-503e368f4d30" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265781 1145 "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x+2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265783 13194139533917 true] [17592186265782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265784 13194139533917 true] [17592186265782 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533917 true] [17592186265782 1041 "field-1" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265782 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265784 13194139533917 true] [17592186265782 1110 #uuid "89517520-5831-5f1e-9edb-49bc2f2a025d" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265783 1034 "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265783 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265783 1111 #uuid "89bfcde6-510f-520f-a35f-be09e4e13a54" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265784 1034 "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265784 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533917 true] [17592186265784 1111 #uuid "723224dc-4baa-565e-b25a-1eafdd8e5a4f" 13194139533917 true] [17592186265785 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265786 13194139533917 true] [1759218